{"author":{"id":"6d85cf2fcebaeefd8c5c528f2d5f5f98f26ce3275422d0dc5f7c0fd596f9f59f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791711374,"nonce":"C7hZ3mZs45jzeMGnAyIQ0V2FkF8=","title":"Text out of its clip is shaped every frame","message":"Every text node in the tree is passed to `text::prepare`, shaped and its glyphs rasterized on every display-list preparation, wherever it is. Only then are its glyph records rejected one by one against the screen clip. A long page in a scroll container shapes thousands of runs per frame to draw a few dozen. The text layout cache, at 256 entries, then thrashes on every frame.\n\nMeasured in a wiki of about 1,000 to 2,700 nodes per page, mostly code blocks of token runs (1120x780, Apple M-series, release):\n- Native preparation of one scroll frame: 10.6 ms -\u003e 0.9 ms on a 1,400-node page; 11.7 -\u003e 1.8 ms on 2,700 nodes.\n- Every page at four scroll depths: 946 -\u003e 631 ms total frame time.\n- On a slower Linux box (Iris Xe, a heavy build running alongside), opening the heaviest page took one 1.2 s frame.\n\nFix (branch text-cull): before shaping, `text_outside_clip` grows the node's box on every side by twice the font size, for ink that overhangs it, plus its decorations' thickness and offset. It tests that box with the very check its glyphs would each be rejected by (`Primitive::outside_clip` against the same `clipping`). Only when the box lies wholly outside is the run skipped. Culling must be on, as for glyphs.\n\nOutput is unchanged, checked on every page of the wiki at four scroll depths, against main:\n- A fresh process renders every page identically with and without the change.\n- Without it, a process that had already rendered about 26 pages drew visible glyphs differently from a fresh one: up to 157 levels per channel on glyph edges. That is a separate history-dependent glyph bug that this change happens to avoid, by not shaping thousands of off-screen runs.\n- With it, every static page matches its fresh render after the whole run. Only animated elements differ, as their clocks do.\n\nTest: `scene::tests::text_out_of_its_clip_is_not_shaped_until_it_scrolls_into_view` checks the atlas. Text below a clip adds no glyphs, and scrolling to it brings an atlas update. With the check disabled the text is rasterized while hidden and the test fails.\n\nWhen text shadows are added, their offset and blur must join the reach, or a shadow that falls into view from text out of it would be dropped.","files":null}]}